From cabf029fdb7a0f6a01ec69641fd11c237e508621 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: root <root@ubuntu.localdomain>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:13:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] feat(rlharness): RESET/StageStart with deterministic
 per-seed stage entry

RESET <stage> <difficulty> <team> <seed> [practice] leaves the title screen and
enters a stage, following TitleScreen::StartDeveloperReplayIfRequested()
(TitleScreen.cpp:188-247): set the GameManager fields, switch the supervisor to
GameManager, then pump until gameplaySetupState == COMPLETE and the setup
subthread is gone.

Determinism required two corrections, both found by diffing the 25 exported
fields between two runs of the same seed instead of guessing:

1. The setup runs on a subthread while the main loop keeps pumping frames, so the
   two threads interleave draws from the shared RNG.  Seeding only inside
   GameManager's setup (before stageRngSeed is recorded) is not enough: the RNG
   state on exit differed run to run (15169 vs 20883).  RESET now reseeds at the
   quiescent point - setup complete, subthread gone, main thread in control.

2. The harness frame counter is process-wide and the setup needs a
   scheduler-dependent number of frames, so field 0 of the tracked set differed
   between runs even when the game state matched.  ResetFrameCount() rewinds it
   at stage start, making frame 0 the first frame of the stage - which is also
   what the replay trace's field 0 means.

Result: two processes, same seed, same 2,500-chunk input plan, produce identical
hash sequences over the 330 frames an episode currently survives (34 samples).
Both runs also end on the same frame.

Also reports game_over explicitly from STEP (g_GameManager.showRetryMenu).

Known limitation: the episode ends at ~frame 329 with exit=1 and game_over=0.
That is the title screen's demo replay chain ending, not a death.  Removing it
properly needs the title template + spawn that P0-WP5 adds; Chain::Cut takes a
specific ChainElem, which the harness does not have a handle to.  Until then
RESET is deterministic but short-lived.
